.TH ROOST 1 "2026-08-02" "roost 0.5.0" "User Commands" \" x-release-please-version
.SH NAME
roost \- top for Claude Code: live sessions, context use, and their subagents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B roost
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B roost
shows every live Claude Code session on the machine, the model each is running,
how much of its context window it has consumed, how long it has been idle, and
the subagents it has spawned. It refreshes in place, like
.BR top (1).
.PP
It reads three local sources. The only file it writes is its own action log,
described under
.B LOGGING
below. There is no network traffic beyond a localhost probe of the inference
ports.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.I ~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json
\(em live sessions: pid, sessionId, launch directory, name.
.IP \(bu 2
.I ~/.claude/projects/*/<sid>.jsonl
\(em model in use and token usage.
.IP \(bu 2
127.0.0.1 ports \(em ollama, litellm, openwebui.
.RE
.PP
The worker table's
.B FLOW
column is an ASCII sparkline of each session's recent token throughput,
normalised to its own busiest sample, newest at the right; a dot is a sample
with no flow. History begins when roost starts and nothing persists. The
subagent table's
.B CTX
column reads tokens over the inferred window (48k/200k), and its
.B AGENT
column shows the agent's type (Explore/a812a) once the parent has recorded
it, falling back to the hex id while the agent is still running.
.PP
Sessions are grouped by what it costs to ignore them rather than by size.
.B NEAR LIMIT
is about to stop working,
.B PARKED + COSTLY
bills its whole context on the next turn, and
.B QUIET
collapses to a single line until a cursor is raised.
.PP
The
.B TREND
column is how much context the session has added over its last few turns, read
back out of the transcript rather than accumulated while roost runs \(em so it
is populated on the first frame and under
.BR \-\-once .
It is an amount rather than a shape or an arrow because context inside a
session only ever rises: it falls solely on
.BR /compact ,
so a sparkline of context would draw the same ramp on every row. A session
creeping up 2k a turn and one adding 22k look identical in
.B CTX
and differ here. It is not a second reading of
.BR FLOW ,
which sparks throughput sampled while roost runs and starts empty; this is an
amount already in the transcript when roost opens it.
.PP
The last line totals the fleet: sessions, context held across all of them,
context added over the same window, and how many are near their limit. Context
held is what a sweep would reclaim. Percentages are deliberately not totalled,
being fractions of different windows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-watch " " [\fISECS\fR]
Refresh interval in seconds. Live is the default; this sets the pace.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Print a single frame and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit session and infra records as JSON, then exit. Intended for piping.
.TP
.B \-\-advise
Start with the ADVICE panel open. Toggle it live with
.BR a .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-agents
Start with the SUBAGENTS panel closed. Toggle it live with
.BR s .
.TP
.B \-\-models
Start with the LOCAL MODELS panel open. Toggle it live with
.BR m .
.TP
.B \-\-usage
Start with the USAGE panel open. Toggle it live with
.BR u .
Set
.B ROOST_WEEKLY_BUDGET
(e.g. 60M) to show the week's cloud burn against a budget; calibrate the
number from Claude Code's /usage once. The tally is an estimate read from
transcripts, not the real Anthropic meter; models without a claude\- name
are flagged (local) and excluded from the budget math.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive
Start with interactive mode armed \(em the cursor,
.BR x / y ,
and the
.B EXPERIMENTAL
marker. Off by default. Toggle it live with
.BR i .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour. Colour is also suppressed when
.B NO_COLOR
is set in the environment or when output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-log
Do not record stopped sessions. See
.B LOGGING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B space
Repaint immediately instead of waiting out the interval.
.TP
.BR a ", " s ", " m ", " u ", " h
Show the ADVICE, SUBAGENTS, LOCAL MODELS, USAGE, or HELP panel; pressing the key
again closes it. Only one panel is open at a time, so asking for one closes
the others. Opening more than one used to push the rest past the bottom of
the terminal, which looked like the key had done nothing.
.TP
.BR h ", " ?
Show the HELP panel: a one-line-each rundown of what each screen on the
display means, not a keybinding reference (the footer hint below the title
already lists the keys).
.TP
.B i
Arm or disarm interactive mode. Off by default; arming it is what turns on
the cursor,
.BR x / y ,
and the
.B EXPERIMENTAL
marker, all together. Disarming drops the cursor and stops
.BR j / k / x / y
from responding until armed again.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " Down ", " Up
Raise the cursor and move it. Requires interactive mode armed (see
.BR i
above). Raising the cursor expands the
.B QUIET
group, since idle sessions are usually the ones worth acting on.
.TP
.B x
Stop the selected session. Requires interactive mode armed. Prompts for
confirmation; only
.B y
proceeds, and every other key cancels.
.TP
.B y
Requires interactive mode armed. Copy the selected session's sessionId to the
clipboard, for
.BR "claude \-\-resume" .
Uses
.BR clip (1)
on Windows,
.BR pbcopy (1)
on macOS and
.BR xclip (1)
elsewhere; a missing helper is reported rather than silently ignored.
.TP
.B Esc
Drop the cursor and re-collapse the QUIET group.
.TP
.B q
Quit. During a confirmation prompt,
.B q
cancels the prompt instead, so that a reflexive quit is never read as consent.
.SH STOPPING A SESSION
.B x
ends a process; it does not compact, save, or otherwise negotiate with the
session. There is no local control channel into a running Claude Code session,
so nothing finer is available from outside it.
.PP
On Unix the session is sent
.B SIGTERM
and exits on its own terms. On Windows there is no cross-process equivalent, so
.B TerminateProcess
is used: the session stops immediately with no chance to flush. Transcripts are
written a turn at a time, so at most a turn already in flight is lost.
.PP
roost refuses to stop its own process or its parent. Run from inside the session
it is pointed at, the cursor can land on the row whose process owns the
terminal, and stopping it would take roost with it.
.SH LOGGING
Every session stopped with
.B x
appends one JSON object to
.IR ~/.claude/logs/roost.jsonl :
timestamp, host, session name, pid, sessionId, model, context tokens held, idle
seconds, and whether the stop succeeded. The file is held at 5000 lines.
.PP
The session's task text is deliberately not recorded. It is free-form prose out
of a transcript, and an audit trail of what was stopped should not become a copy
of what was being worked on.
.PP
Because the record carries the context each session was holding, the log answers
afterwards what a sweep actually reclaimed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
python3 \-c 'import json,sys; print(sum(json.loads(l)["ctx_tokens"] or 0
  for l in open(sys.argv[1]) if json.loads(l)["ok"]))' ~/.claude/logs/roost.jsonl
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pass
.B \-\-no\-log
to record nothing. A log that cannot be written is ignored rather than raised:
losing the log is survivable, losing the display is not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B roost
exits 0 unless it could not parse its arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set to any value, colour output is disabled. See
.UR https://no-color.org
.UE .
.SH CAVEATS
roost reads an undocumented on-disk format that can change without warning.
Treat breakage as expected rather than exceptional.
.PP
The context window is inferred, not recorded: nothing on disk states which window
a session opened with, so roost picks the smallest standard tier the observed
usage fits inside. A session on the 1M window can read 480k cache tokens in a
single call, and scoring that against 200k yields a nonsense reading.
.PP
Context itself is an estimate \(em the last assistant turn's input, cache-read
and cache-creation tokens summed over that window.
.PP
.B TREND
on an idle session describes when it was last working, not now. Read it beside
.BR IDLE :
a large figure on a session idle an hour says it grew fast an hour ago. It also
spans however many turns are left in the transcript tail, so a young session
shows a shorter span than an old one and a session with one turn shows
.BR \- .
.PP
The ADVICE panel's thresholds are tuned to one person's usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/sessions/
One JSON file per live session, named for its pid.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
Session transcripts, and subagent transcripts one directory deeper.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/logs/roost.jsonl
One JSON record per session stopped. The only file roost writes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR claude (1)
.SH BUGS
Report at
